Zero installed-folder UEFI x64 loader verification - 2026-11-17

PASS: actual QEMU/OVMF full Zero kernel boot from sibling system.iso in a
private FAT directory, with no CD-ROM or ISO virtual optical device attached.
The fixture was produced by native zero-setup deploy; the test added a copy
of ZEROX64.EFI at the firmware-standard EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI fallback name.
The loader was still in a nested directory and resolved its own sibling ISO.
The terminal executed native Zero (42 -> ASCII Z), Ezero arithmetic (42) and
Ezero host() (0), proving both the kernel and compiler bundle were loaded.

PASS: five additional actual firmware runs refused malformed ISO input before
ExitBootServices: truncated length, altered PVD volume length, altered root
extent, altered kernel member extent and invalid compiler-bundle header.

PASS: independent native execution of the exact authored Zero parser blocks:
16 path cases plus10 ISO metadata cases, including Unicode, multiple FILEPATH
nodes, maximum accepted path, overlong/unterminated/malformed nodes, adjacent
buffer canaries, paired-endian mismatch, missing members and catalog damage.
Evidence is in ../review/results.json and ../review/check-parser.py.

All6 firmware tests stopped their QEMU process. The original build/data.img,
build/data.vdi and build/zero.iso remained hash-identical throughout the final
test run. User data disks were never attached. Private FAT payloads were also
hash-identical after each run; QEMU used read-only backing with a private
snapshot. No host boot configuration was modified.

Loader source SHA256:
d5ae3ac753ee5004cccedfc6071085c0a5ba48208ee5c204b347e3f8f1570706
EFI binary size24576, SHA256:
29c3f0ec66936228671eb348d4259e259cbcc639f09f86c40a7288f9c62636ef
Tested current ISO SHA256:
2c15dbc7e8f058c5ba07024c4c6c1ddd1ffcfcd86d15a2cc7c49ca7fbfd4a3df

The same EFI bytes were emitted by the frozen0.10 compiler and the current
compiler with new host durability operations. No assembler or foreign
compiler is used by the product implementation.

Scope: UEFI x64 and a firmware-readable filesystem, normally FAT. This does
not implement arbitrary NTFS/ext4/Btrfs host-folder boot, storage.data as a
writable kernel block device, boot-menu registration, BIOS/IA32 directory
boot, signature verification or unattended recovery. It is a real new early
loader step toward installed boot, not complete Wubi-style installation.
